# local.rules -- custom Snort rules I authored for the detection lab.
# Each was tested by generating the matching attack from a Kali box and
# confirming the alert fired on the sensor console. See ../README.md for the
# walkthrough and ../evidence/ for the console captures of these firing.
# Addresses are placeholders; $HOME_NET / $EXTERNAL_NET are snort.conf variables.
# The two locally authored scan signatures are sid:1000006 and sid:1000010.
# The large-ICMP rule is kept with the lab set because the ping evidence proves
# that payload-size matching fired on the sensor.

# --- Reconnaissance -------------------------------------------------------

# Xmas-tree scan: FIN, PSH and URG set together is never a legitimate packet,
# only a scanner sends it. Generated with `nmap -sX`.
alert tcp any any -> any any (msg:"Nmap XMAS Tree Scan"; flags:FPU; sid:1000006; rev:1;)

# UDP scan. Generated with `nmap -sU`.
alert udp any any -> any any (msg:"Nmap UDP Scan"; sid:1000010; rev:1;)

# --- Volumetric / abuse ---------------------------------------------------

# Oversized ICMP: a normal ping is a few dozen bytes; >800 is a flood or
# tunneling attempt. Generated with `ping -s 1400`.
alert icmp $EXTERNAL_NET any -> $HOME_NET any (msg:"MISC Large ICMP Packet"; dsize:>800; reference:arachnids,246; classtype:bad-unknown; sid:499; rev:1;)

# --- Logging (not alerting) ----------------------------------------------

# Log inbound DNS from outside for later analysis rather than raising an alert.
log udp $EXTERNAL_NET any -> $HOME_NET 53 (msg:"DNS query DoS"; classtype:attempted-dos; sid:1000004; rev:1;)

# --- Host-scoped watch ----------------------------------------------------

# Watch all inbound connections to a single monitored host.
alert tcp any any -> 10.10.0.10 any (msg:"Inbound to monitored host"; sid:1000020; rev:1;)
